# Publify's core models declared string attributes whose length only
# MySQL's implicit 255-character column limit bounded -- on PostgreSQL
# and other databases the values persisted unbounded, so any form that
# mass-assigns a string column could store an arbitrarily large value
# the application later pays to load and render (improper input
# validation, CWE-20; the release sweep bounded every string attribute
# at once). The vulnerable models under app/models/ name their string
# attributes only in presence and uniqueness validations. Commit
# 34f6e9c98e ("Validate lengths of string attributes", released in
# 9.2.10) adds the concern app/models/concerns/string_length_limit.rb
# and applies it across the models:
#
#   STRING_LIMIT = 255
#   validates_default_string_length :email, :text_filter_name
#
# with a different explicit bound where a column has one. The pool row
# already names this repository -- the advisory names publify/publify,
# which vendors publify_core as a directory, and the row points at the
# repository holding the fixing commit.
#
# Held: cross-database attribute budgets. No binding labels a Rails
# `validates` declaration in either direction, and none can: the Ruby
# frontend emits no call-argument tokens, and every symbol argument
# (`:email`, `:login`) lowers to an anonymous const with no path, so
# which attribute a validation bounds is unanswerable from the graph.
# The one in-file route -- a model class whose file carries no length
# atom -- is file-wide rather than class-confined in Ruby and does not
# discriminate: on the fixing revision it still reports Article, Page
# and Comment, whose bound arrives through the StringLengthLimit
# concern inherited from Content and Feedback, and across ordinary
# Rails code it reports 35 of 72 validating classes in one application
# and 2 of 2 in another that carry no such weakness. Unbounded text is
# also the correct declaration for genuinely long fields. Both blocks
# assert reject VYQL-DOS-029: when attribute-budget vocabulary lands,
# the unvalidated form must flip to expect.

test "rank1616: string attributes carry no length validation"
  lang ruby
  file publify_core/app/models/user.rb
  reject VYQL-DOS-029
  code
  ```
  class User < ApplicationRecord
    devise :database_authenticatable, :registerable,
           :recoverable, :rememberable, :trackable, :validatable
    include ConfigManager

    before_validation :set_default_profile

    validates :login, uniqueness: true
    validates :email, :login, presence: true
    validates :login, length: { in: 3..40 }

    belongs_to :resource, optional: true
    has_many :notifications, foreign_key: "notify_user_id"

    serialize :settings, Hash

    def permalink_url(anchor)
      "#{blog.base_url}authors/#{login}##{anchor}"
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1616: the fixed revision bounds the string attributes"
  lang ruby
  file publify_core/app/models/user.rb
  reject VYQL-DOS-029
  code
  ```
  class User < ApplicationRecord
    devise :database_authenticatable, :registerable,
           :recoverable, :rememberable, :trackable, :validatable
    include ConfigManager
    include StringLengthLimit

    before_validation :set_default_profile

    validates :login, uniqueness: true
    validates :email, :login, presence: true
    validates :login, length: { in: 3..40 }
    validates_default_string_length :email, :text_filter_name

    belongs_to :resource, optional: true
    has_many :notifications, foreign_key: "notify_user_id"

    serialize :settings, Hash

    def permalink_url(anchor)
      "#{blog.base_url}authors/#{login}##{anchor}"
    end
  end

  module StringLengthLimit
    # Default string length limit for model attributes. When running
    # on MySQL, this is equal to the default string length in the
    # database as set by Rails.
    STRING_LIMIT = 255

    extend ActiveSupport::Concern

    class_methods do
      def validates_default_string_length(*names)
        names.each do |name|
          validates name, length: { maximum: STRING_LIMIT }
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```
